.TH KEYPARCEL 1 "2026-10-19" "keyparcel" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
keyparcel \- explicitly share encrypted text and files through your SSH server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keyparcel
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B keyparcel help
.br
.B keyparcel version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Devices use the same SSH account as an inbox for encrypted, signed parcels.
Only the clients need keyparcel installed; the server needs SSH/SFTP.
There is no continuous clipboard monitoring or synchronization.
The server can observe encrypted sizes, timing and recipient inboxes.
.PP
Each device needs its own readable, unencrypted Ed25519 or RSA private key
(RSA at least 2048 bits). Passphrase-protected keys, certificates and
hardware/agent-only identities are not supported.
Enroll the public key on the server separately; setup does not upload it.
Compare the server fingerprint and each peer fingerprint through a trusted
channel before approval. SSH key comments are not proof of identity.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B version
Print build version and source revision. Also available as \fB\-\-version\fR.
.TP
.B help
Print command usage. Also available as \fB\-\-help\fR or with no arguments.
.TP
.B setup \-\-host TARGET [\-\-identity PATH] [\-\-default] [\-\-root PATH] [\-\-discovery PATH]
Save a profile for user@host or an OpenSSH alias. The identity defaults to
\fI~/.ssh/id_ed25519\fR; it is not generated. \fB\-\-default\fR selects the
profile for commands without \fB\-\-host\fR. Remote paths default to
\fI.local/share/keyparcel\fR and \fI.ssh/authorized_keys\fR, relative to the
server account's home. All devices must use the same paths.
.TP
.B devices [\-\-json]
Discover enrolled devices and show their fingerprints and local approval state.
Discovery alone does not approve a peer.
.TP
.B trust FINGERPRINT \-\-name NAME
Approve a currently discovered key under a unique local name after independently
checking its fingerprint. Explicitly untrust an old name before replacing it.
.TP
.B untrust NAME
Remove the local peer approval. This does not revoke the key on the SSH server.
.TP
.B send \-\-to NAME [\-\-to NAME ...] (\-\-text TEXT | \-\-stdin | \-\-file PATH)
Send one parcel to each approved recipient. Select exactly one input:
literal text, standard input as text, or a regular file. Whitespace and empty
input are preserved. Files retain their original basename in encrypted metadata.
Partial delivery can succeed for some recipients while the command fails.
Check recipient outcomes; an unconfirmed delivery must be checked before resending.
.TP
.B inbox [\-\-json | \-\-watch [\-\-interval DURATION]]
List parcels whose metadata verifies for the current identity and approvals.
Rejected packages are reported on standard error and give a nonzero exit status,
even when accepted items are also shown. Uploaded times are approximate,
unverified server file times, displayed newest first in the local timezone.
\fB\-\-watch\fR polls using a persistent connection; the positive interval
defaults to \fB2s\fR. Watch and JSON output cannot be combined.
.TP
.B receive ID (\-\-stdout | \-\-copy | \-\-save\-to PATH) [\-\-keep] [\-\-overwrite]
Verify the sender signature, recipient, transfer ID, complete content hash and
length before delivering any content. IDs are 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters;
a unique prefix of 8 through 31 characters is also accepted.
Choose exactly one output action, described below.
.TP
.B delete ID [ID ...]
Delete this identity's remote inbox copies without downloading or decrypting them.
Full IDs are deleted directly; unique prefixes require an inbox listing.
Other recipients' copies and local saved files
are unaffected. The command continues past individual failures and reports a count.
.TP
.B delete \-\-all
Delete all completed parcel filenames in the current identity's inbox on the
selected host. Cannot be combined with IDs; this is not a sender-trust filter.
.TP
.B bench [\-\-rounds N]
Run diagnostic transfer measurements against the selected server, including
a temporary self-addressed parcel that is removed after successful receipt.
The default is 20 rounds; the supported range is 1 through 1000.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
Operational commands accept the following options; setup saves its profile values.
.TP
.B \-\-config PATH
Use a different local JSON configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-host TARGET
Select a saved SSH profile, or the target to save with setup.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-config PATH
Use an explicit OpenSSH configuration file for remote operations.
Otherwise normal OpenSSH configuration is used. Interactive first connections
use OpenSSH's host-key prompt and known_hosts; changed keys are rejected.
Unattended commands and completion require an already trusted server key.
.TP
.B \-\-access\-method auto|ssh|sftp
Override remote read access for this invocation, or save the choice with setup.
The default/saved \fBauto\fR and explicit \fBssh\fR require supported Unix
commands in the CLI. If unavailable, use \fB\-\-access\-method sftp\fR.
\fBsftp\fR uses only SFTP. GUI Automatic instead probes once per connection
and falls back to SFTP when command access is unsupported.
Uploads and mutations remain on SFTP for every choice; there is no retry of a
partially started read through another method.
.SH RECEIVE OUTPUT AND RETENTION
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Write the full verified original bytes to standard output, including file parcels
and binary data. There is no GUI preview or clipboard size limit on this action.
.TP
.B \-\-copy
Copy full verified UTF-8 text without NUL bytes, including eligible file parcels,
up to and including 16 MiB (16,777,216 bytes). Linux requires \fBwl-copy\fR
or \fBxclip\fR; macOS uses its system clipboard runtime. Validation or clipboard
failure retains the remote parcel. Rich-text formats are not interpreted.
.TP
.B \-\-save\-to PATH
Save complete verified bytes to an exact filename or an existing directory.
Directory saves use the original filename for files or \fITRANSFER-ID.txt\fR
for text. Parent directories must exist. There is no clipboard or preview size cap.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
With \fB\-\-save\-to\fR, replace an existing regular output file atomically.
Symlink and nonregular outputs are rejected. Without this option an existing
output file is not replaced.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Retain the remote copy after successful receipt. Otherwise CLI receive removes it
only after successful output. Verification/output failure retains it; a failure
removing the remote copy can occur after local output has already succeeded.
GUI receive/save retains the remote copy until explicit removal.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
The CLI packages include Bash and zsh completion. Source the supplied
\fIcompletions/keyparcel.bash\fR in Bash, or install \fIcompletions/_keyparcel\fR
in a zsh fpath directory and enable \fBautoload -Uz compinit; compinit\fR.
For a user-local zsh install, use \fI~/.local/share/zsh/site-functions/_keyparcel\fR
and add its directory to fpath before compinit. Startup files are not edited.
.PP
Suggestions share the CLI's read-only completion API. Local filenames use native
shell completion. Remote queries are noninteractive, silent on failure, and
limited to five seconds. Receive/delete suggestions are full IDs from filenames;
actual receipt still verifies the parcel and sender before exposing any content.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/keyparcel/config.json
Linux configuration when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set; otherwise
\fI~/.config/keyparcel/config.json\fR. The CLI and Linux GTK client share profiles
and local peer approvals.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/keyparcel/config.json
macOS default CLI configuration location.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config, ~/.ssh/known_hosts
Normal OpenSSH configuration and server trust stores, subject to OpenSSH options.
.PP
Private identity files are user-managed, not package-owned. Losing an identity can
make existing parcels permanently unreadable; there is no identity recovery.
Removing packages does not remove user configuration, identities or saved files.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a dedicated identity; enroll its .pub file separately on the server:
.PP
.nf
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f ~/.ssh/keyparcel -C laptop
keyparcel setup --host parcel-vps --identity ~/.ssh/keyparcel --default
keyparcel devices
keyparcel trust SHA256:FINGERPRINT --name phone
keyparcel send --to phone --text 'hello'
keyparcel send --to phone --file ./report.pdf
keyparcel inbox
keyparcel receive TRANSFER-ID --save-to ~/Downloads --keep
keyparcel receive TRANSFER-ID --stdout > parcel.bin
keyparcel receive TRANSFER-ID --copy
keyparcel delete TRANSFER-ID
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success (including help and version output).
One indicates an application, validation or operational error. The command can
have produced output or completed some recipient operations before an error;
inspect its diagnostics before retrying. No finer application exit-code scheme
is defined. Signal termination may have the shell's normal signal status.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh-keygen (1),
.BR sftp (1)
.PP
Installation, enrollment and security documentation:
https://github.com/keyparcel/keyparcel
